<clickhouse>
  <!--
    Memory guardrails for a CO-LOCATED deployment, where ClickHouse shares a host with the
    backend, Keycloak, Postgres and Redis.

    ClickHouse's defaults assume a dedicated machine. Measured on a 7.65 GiB host they resolved to:
      max_server_memory_usage   6.89 GiB  (90% of the host)
      mark_cache_size           3.83 GiB
      uncompressed_cache_size   3.83 GiB
    The two caches alone were budgeted at more than the whole machine. Under concurrent dashboard
    load this killed the server repeatedly.

    The memory ratio is relative, not absolute. ClickHouse applies it to the smaller of host RAM
    and the container's memory limit, so size ClickHouse with CLICKHOUSE_MEM_LIMIT in .env. Do not
    edit this file: upgrades replace it.

    With this ceiling in place and no concurrency caps, ClickHouse survived 10, 25 and 50
    concurrent pairs of heavy dashboard and chart queries with no restarts. Queries that would
    exceed the ceiling fail with Code 241 instead of taking the server down.

    Concurrency caps (max_concurrent_queries and the per-kind select and insert caps) are
    deliberately NOT set. ClickHouse enforces them by rejecting excess queries immediately with
    Code 202; it does not queue them. In testing that turned dashboard loads into HTTP 500s,
    failed logins into HTTP 500s, and ingestion inserts into retries. queue_max_wait_ms makes
    queries wait only for the global max_concurrent_queries pool, and only for callers that pass
    it, so admission control with queueing needs backend and Fluent Bit changes and belongs in a
    later release.

    Per-query limits (max_memory_usage, max_threads) are profile settings and cannot live here,
    because ClickHouse loads profiles only from users.xml. The backend can pass them per query.
  -->

  <!-- Fraction of min(host RAM, container memory limit) that ClickHouse may use. -->
  <max_server_memory_usage_to_ram_ratio>0.70</max_server_memory_usage_to_ram_ratio>

  <!--
    Caches. Defaults are auto-sized to a fraction of host RAM, which is far too generous when
    co-located. The uncompressed cache is disabled outright because use_uncompressed_cache
    defaults to 0, so it was reserving memory it could never use.
  -->
  <mark_cache_size>536870912</mark_cache_size>
  <index_mark_cache_size>268435456</index_mark_cache_size>
  <uncompressed_cache_size>0</uncompressed_cache_size>
  <index_uncompressed_cache_size>0</index_uncompressed_cache_size>
</clickhouse>
